    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-10424" textLabel="MONOMER-10424" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q6PMT1" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="745.9051513671875" centerY="869.0743408203125" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">5-Methylthioribose kinases catalyze the phosphorylation of the C-1 hydroxyl group of the ribose moiety of methylthioribose (MTR) to yield 5-methylthioribose-1-phosphate. The MTR kinase, MTK1 encoded by the rice &lt;i&gt;MTK1&lt;/i&gt; gene has been expressed in &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; and purified for kinetic analysis. The specific activity of the purified enzyme was determined to be 19.4 nmol.min&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt;.mg&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; of enzyme; a factor much lower (3- to 30-fold less) than that found for bacterial MTR kinases. A plot of substrate versus velocity yielded a sigmoidal-shaped rather than a parabolic curve that suggested cooperative enzyme kinetics. The resulting K&lt;sub&gt;0.5&lt;/sub&gt; for MTR at ATP = 4 mM was estimated at 16.8 &amp;mu;M, while K&lt;sub&gt;0.5&lt;/sub&gt; for ATP at MTR = 100 &amp;mu;M was 184 &amp;mu;M |CITS:[15557090]|. &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt;, regulation of rice &lt;i&gt;MTK&lt;/i&gt; genes was linked to sulfur metabolism, where very high gene induction was noted in sulfur-deprived plants (while omission of iron or nitrogen from the nutrient solution resulted in slight down-regulation of those genes) |CITS:[15557090]|.</Comment>
